  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nNext week is looking to remain dry, with upper level subsidence\nsetting in as the ridge moves east over the central CONUS.\nCurrent ensemble guidance is in agreement that once this\nanomalous ridge sets in over the central CONUS, it will remain\nover the region keeping the above-average temperatures around\nthrough at least Friday. The 850 mb temperatures will be at\nleast in the low to mid 20s C (above 95th climatological\npercentile), translating to surface temperatures in the mid to\nupper 90s. LREF guidance shows there is a chance (30-60%) that\ncentral and east-central MO could reach 100 F or higher, which\ncould be caused by southwesterly downsloping flow off the Ozark\nPlateau. While the temperatures are expected to be above\naverage, perhaps the warmest we've seen all year, the dewpoints\nare anticipated to be notably lower compared to previous\nheatwaves. Long range model guidance indicates dewpoints in the\nlow to mid 60s, with a small chance (20-40%) of dewpoints 70 F\nor greater in northeastern MO and west-central IL. As a result,\nheat index values in the upper 90s are possible with some areas\npeaking to near 100 F by mid week. This means that despite\npotentially warmer temperatures than earlier in the year, this\nheatwave is not expected to be as extreme as those from earlier\nin the summer. There still remains uncertainty of the magnitude\nof the moisture, and how much it will affect the resulting heat\nindex values. Given that, our confidence is low to issue a heat\nheadline for this event.",
